# the argument of hume-dialogues-1.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# S1 reports the sceptics' claim (Hume's Philo); the argument proper runs S2-S4.
# S4's "must remain in suspense" is read as following from the balance, not as a separate modal claim.
atom	invincible	reason, considered in an abstract view, furnishes invincible arguments against itself
atom	too_refined	the sceptical reasonings are so refined and subtle that they cannot counterpoise the arguments derived from the senses and experience
atom	conviction	we retain conviction or assurance on some subject
atom	wide	our arguments lose this advantage and run wide of common life
atom	counter	the most refined scepticism is upon a footing with our arguments and counterbalances them
atom	equal	the one has no more weight than the other
atom	suspense	the mind remains in suspense between them
atom	triumph	scepticism triumphs
P1	S1	invincible ∧ (¬too_refined → ¬conviction)	says sceptics
P2	S2	wide → counter
P3	S3	counter → equal
P4	S4	suspense → triumph	kind conceptual
# Unstated step: when arguments are of equal weight, the mind remains in suspense.
A1	-	equal → suspense
C1	S4	wide → suspense	from P2 P3 A1
C	S4	wide → suspense ∧ triumph	from C1 P4
